STAR bullet guidance:
- Every bullet must explicitly provide situation_task, action, result, final
  text, relevance, provenance, and impact metadata.
- situation_task names the context, need, constraint, or responsibility; action
  states what the candidate did; result states a concrete outcome or downstream
  value. The final text communicates all three in one concise sentence.
- Name what was built, changed, analyzed, tested, integrated, debugged, or
  resolved; do not write responsibility-only or tool-only bullets.
- Make the candidate's contribution and ownership clear, especially for shared
  work. Use qualitative impact only when the source supports the relationship.
- Treat source bullets as evidence, not mandatory output. Select the strongest
  accomplishments, rewrite weak wording, remove redundancy, and order bullets
  by target-role relevance. You may synthesize compatible facts, but never
  combine unrelated accomplishments.
- Keep one central accomplishment per bullet and make bullets complementary
  rather than semantically repetitive.
- Prefer precise action verbs and concrete nouns. Avoid generic AI phrases such
  as "drove strategic initiatives", "improved efficiency", or "delivered
  innovative solutions" unless the sentence explains the supported evidence.
- Preserve verified numbers and technical distinctions. You may add a plausible,
  conservative point estimate grounded in a source fact even when the evidence
  plan did not declare it; keep it interview-defensible.
